    "resolved_from_fallback": true,
    "final_cot": "First describe the image: In the figure, region A on the left is labeled with light energy, thylakoid structure, and H2O entering, and beside it ① represents the substance produced from water and released in the light reaction; region A is labeled ATP and NADPH and has arrows connected with region B on the right. Region B is drawn as a cycle, CO2 enters the cycle, there is 2C3 in the figure, and ④ is located at the receptor position before CO2 fixation; region B also has arrows returning to region A, and ② and ③ correspond to the substances returned to the light reaction after NADPH and ATP are used in the dark reaction. Then reason: Photolysis of water in the light reaction releases O2, so ① is O2; NADPH is oxidized to NADP+ after participating in the dark reaction, so ② is NADP+; ATP is converted to ADP+Pi after supplying energy, so ③ is ADP+Pi; the five-carbon compound that fixes CO2 in the Calvin cycle is C5, so ④ is C5.",
